    "field": "uniform",
    "layout": "even",
    "capacity": 1000
  },
  "arithmetic": {
    "supply": "S = M * r — what a cell offers per step at the fixed point",
    "sequential": "eaters in slot order take min(income, left); the supply pays floor(S/t) of them in full and the next gets the remainder, so the population saturates at cells*k_seq and does not grow with n",
    "proportional": "eaters split ONE income: taken = income / k, so the crowd is limited by the demand side, k_prop = floor(income/m)",
    "survival": "an organism survives iff income / k >= m — non-strict: measured `taken == m` keeps energy at its starting 10.0",
    "why_different": "S/t is about the field, income/m is about the organism — they are different quantities and predict different capacities",
    "cliff": "proportional falls off a cliff at n = cells*k_prop: everyone on a cell shares equally, so all live or all die together",
    "boundary": "M*r >= income — below it the cell supplies less than one eater asks and k_seq = 0 by construction. Every row here is at or above the boundary; the boundary itself is stated, not crossed",
    "sequential_reference": "the control compares the measured arm against cells*(floor(S/t) + [S - floor(S/t)*t >= m]) computed by `reference_k_seq`, written separately from `k_sequential` so the two can disagree; a disagreement turns `silent` red",
    "probes": "probe_floor_div and probe_k_prop ran before the ladder and passed: floor_div(0.6, 0.1) == 6 and k_proportional(0.6, 0.1) == 6, not the 5 that `0.6 // 0.1` gives. A failure here refuses the run with exit 2 instead of writing a file",
    "boundary_values": [
      {
        "M": 2.0,
        "r": 0.8,
        "income": 0.6,
        "maintenance": 0.1,
        "S": 1.6,
        "above": true
      },
      {
        "M": 2.0,
        "r": 0.8,
        "income": 1.0,
        "maintenance": 0.1,
        "S": 1.6,
        "above": true
      },
      {
        "M": 2.0,
        "r": 0.8,
        "income": 0.6,
        "maintenance": 0.5,
        "S": 1.6,
        "above": true
      }
    ],
    "k_prop_is_not_income_over_m_floor": "floor(income/m) is the integer law, but the float floor gives 5 for 0.6/0.1 and the run gives 6: the sixth eater takes the 0.1 left on the cell. `floor_div` carries a relative epsilon"
  },
